course: dictionary
lesson: 3296
title: Where the river meets the sea
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An estuary is a noun, a naming word for a place. It is the wide part of a river where it meets the sea, and fresh river water mixes with salty sea water there. «The Thames reaches the North Sea through London and a wide estuary.» The word wide is a clue: the river spreads out as it nears the sea.
explain: Use the words around it to check. «A river starts at its source, is joined by smaller streams called tributaries, and ends at its mouth; where a river meets the sea and the tide comes in, the wide part is called an estuary.» The sentence tells you where it is (where a river meets the sea), what happens there (the tide comes in) and what the wide part is called.
explain: Estuary can also mean a way of speaking. Estuary English is common around London and the Thames estuary. A word can have more than one meaning, so the context tells you which one fits. If the text is about rivers and tides, it means the place. If it is about accents or how people talk, it means the way of speaking.
fact: An estuary is the wide part of a river where it meets the sea.
fact: In an estuary, fresh water and salty sea water mix.
fact: Estuaries have tides, mud flats and many birds.
fact: «Estuary» is a noun, and its plural is «estuaries».
fact: «Estuary English» is a way of speaking common around London and the Thames estuary.
exercise: What is an estuary? | A kind of boat used on rivers | A small stream that joins a river | *A wide part of a river where it meets the sea | A tall hill where a river starts
exercise: In «reaches the North Sea through London and a wide estuary», what does the estuary connect the Thames to? | Windsor | The Cotswold hills | Kemble | *The North Sea
exercise: What is mixed together in an estuary? | Oil and milk | *Fresh water and salty sea water | Ice and sand | Hot rock and cold rock
test: What do we call the smaller streams that join a river, as in «a river is joined by smaller streams called tributaries»? | tides | *tributaries | estuaries | mud flats
test: Where would you find mud flats, tides and many birds in geography? | On a mountain top | In a desert | *In an estuary | In a city park
exam: Which meaning fits «Estuary English» best? | A kind of seaside bird | A tall hill near a river's source | *A way of speaking common around London and the Thames estuary | The mouth of a river where fresh water meets the sea
